To set up Laravel Dusk on an AWS EC2 instance, follow these steps:

1. Install Laravel and its dependencies on the EC2 instance by running the following commands:

   ```
   sudo apt-get update
   sudo apt-get install curl -y
   curl -sL https://laravel.build/example-app | bash
   cd example-app
   composer install
   ```

2. Install PHP extensions required by Laravel Dusk:

   ```
   sudo apt-get update
   sudo apt-get install php7.4-gd php7.4-zip php7.4-mbstring php7.4-bcmath php7.4-intl -y
   ```

3. Install Laravel Dusk:

   ```
   composer require --dev laravel/dusk
   ```

4. Create a new directory in your project's `app` directory named `Scenario`s or follow Laravel's default naming convention:

   ```
   mkdir app/Tests/Dusk/Scenarios
   ```

5. Create a new `DuskTestCase` file for your Dusk tests:

   ```
   php artisan make:dusk
   ```

   This command creates the `Tests\Browser\TestCase.php` file.

6. Update your `TestCase.php` to extend the `DuskTestCase`:

   ```
   <?php

   namespace Tests\Browser;

   use App\Nova\Foundation;
   use Illuminate\Foundation\Testing\WithoutMiddleware;
   use Laravel\Dusk\Concerns\InteractsWithDusk;
   use Laravel\Dusk\TestCase as BaseTestCase;
   use Facebook\WebDriver\Chrome\ChromeOptions;
   use Facebook\WebDriver\Remote\RemoteWebDriver;
   use Facebook\WebDriverieurs\User;

   class TestCase extends BaseTestCase
   {
       use InteractsWithDusk;

       protected $assertionsDisabled = false;

       protected function makeBrowser(): RemoteWebDriver
       {
           $options = (new ChromeOptions())->addArguments([
               '--disable-gpu',
               '--headless',
               '--window-size=1600,1200',
           ]);

           return RemoteWebDriver::create('http://localhost:9515', $options, 60);
       }
   }
   ```

7. Create a new dusk test file inside the `app/Tests/Dusk/Scenarios` directory. For example:

   ```
   <?php

   namespace Tests\Browser\Scenarios;

   use Tests\Browser\TestCase;

   class ExampleScenario extends TestCase
   {
       public function testTitle()
       {
           $user = $this->actingAs(User::factory()->create());
           $this->browse(function ($browser) use ($user) {
               $browser->loginAs($user, 'web')
                   ->visit('/')
                   ->assertSee('Laravel');
           });
       }
   }
   ```

8. Update your `.env.dusk.local` file with your AWS EC2 instance's details (e.g., EC2 IP address):

   ```
   BROWSER_DRIVER_URL=http://<Instance_Public_IP>:9515
   ```

9. Run your first Dusk test by executing the following command:

   ```
   php artisan dusk
   ```

   This will execute your tests using the Chrome browser via Selenium.

Note: If you encounter any errors while setting up Dusk, check the Laravel documentation on setting up Dusk for more details: https://laravel.com/docs/9.x/dusk#installation-and-setup. Also, make sure you have installed the necessary browsers and Selenium server/Xvfb dependencies based on your operating system. For AWS EC2, you can use Amazon EC2 Launch Wizards or follow the official installation guide for your preferred Linux distribution: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/download-requests.html#download-requests-preferred-download-methods.

To set up Laravel Dusk on an AWS EC2 instance, follow these steps:

1. Create an EC2 instance with an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) of your choice. Ensure the instance has a public IP address and the necessary security settings to allow incoming traffic on ports 22 (SSH) and 80/443 (HTTP/HTTPS) if you plan on accessing the instance via a web browser.

2. Connect to the EC2 instance via SSH using an SSH client like PuTTY, and run the following commands:

   ```
   sudo apt update
   sudo apt install unzip php libapache2-mod-php php-mysql mysql-server -y
   ```

   These commands will install the necessary packages for Laravel, including Apache web server, PHP, and MySQL.

3. Create a new Laravel project using Composer, or download an existing one. To do so, run these commands:

   ```
   curl -sS https://getcomposer.org/installer | php
   sudo mv composer.phar /usr/local/bin/composer
   composer create-project --prefer-dist laravel/laravel my-app
   ```

4. Inside your Laravel project, create a new file named `DuskServiceProvider.php` in the `app/Providers` folder, with the following contents:

   ```php
   <?php

   namespace App\Providers;

   use Laravel\Dusk\DuskServiceProvider;

   class DuskServiceProvider extends DuskServiceProvider
   {
       protected $namespace = 'App\\Tests\\Browser';

       protected $addonUrls = [
           'https://chrome.google.com/webstore/detail/chrome-devtools-ajax-b36ggbhdijojeaoiedmipjafähnikg' => [
               'name' => 'Chrome DevTools - AJAX',
               'versionRegex' => '^\\d+.\\d+.\\d+$',
           ],
           'https://chrome.google.com/webstore/detail/chrome-devtools-network-m/nbmckjccnaonkcaapfpbgbugahcklqqi' => [
               'name' => 'Chrome DevTools - Network',
               'versionRegex' => '^\\d+.\\d+.\\d+$',
           ],
       ];

       /**
        * Register any application services.
        *
        * @return void
        */
       public function register()
       {
           $this->publishes([
               __DIR__ . '/../.. /config/dusk.php' => config_path('dusk.php'),
           ], 'dusk-config');

           $this->app->singleton('dusk.browser', function () {
               return new \Laravel\Dusk\ChromeBrowser(
                   base_path() . '/vendor/laravel/dusk/bin/chromedriver',
                   ['--no-sandbox', '--headless', '--disable-dev-shm-usage']
               );
           });

           $this->app->alias('dusk.browser', \Laravel\Dusk\Browser::class);

           $this->app->singleton(\Laravel\Dusk\Manager::class, function ($app) {
               $manager = new \Laravel\Dusk\Manager($app);

               $manager->extend('selenium', function () {
                   return \Laravel\Dusk\Selenium::driver();
               });

               return $manager;
           });
       }
   }
   ```

5. Create a new file named `.env` and copy the contents of the `.env.example` file. Update the database connection, app URL, and any other necessary settings.

6. Create a new folder on the server called `resources/tests/Browser` and copy the following `HomePageTest.php` file into it:

   ```php
   <?php

   namespace App\Tests\Browser;

   use App\Tests\Helper\TestBrowser;
   use Illuminate\Foundation\Testing\DatabaseTransactions;
   use Laravel\Dusk\TestCase;
   use Tests\TestCase as BaseTestCase;

   class HomePageTest extends BaseTestCase
   {
       use DatabaseTransactions, TestBrowser;

       public function testPageLoads()
       {
           $this->browse(function (TestBrowser $browser) {
               $browser->visit('/')
                       ->assertSee('Laravel');
           });
       }
   }
   ```

7. In the `phpunit.xml` file, update or add the following section:

   ```xml
   <phpUnit>
     <!-- ... Other settings ... -->

     <testsuites>
       <testsuite name="Browser Tests">
         <directory suffix="TestBrowser.php">/path/to/your/Laravel/project/resources/tests/Browser</directory>
       </testsuite>
     </testsuites>

     <!-- Other settings... -->
   </phpUnit>
   ```

   Ensure to replace `/path/to/your/Laravel/project` with the actual path to your Laravel project.

8. Download and install the necessary browser drivers. For Chrome, run the following command to download the Chrome driver that matches your Chrome version:

   ```
   curl -sS -o /tmp/chromedriver_linux64.zip -L https://chromedriver.storage.googleapis.com/latest/chromedriver_linux64.zip
   unzip -qo /tmp/chromedriver_linux64.zip -d /usr/local/bin/
   rm /tmp/chromedriver_linux64.zip
   ```

9. Install Laravel Dusk packages, run the following command:

   ```
   composer require --dev laravel/dusk
   ```

10. Finally, create a new file called `dusk.json` in the root of your project and insert the following configuration:

   ```json
   {
       "driver": "chrome",
       "chrome": {
           "driver" : "/usr/local/bin/chromedriver",
           "options" : [
               "--no-sandbox",
               "--headless",
               "--disable-dev-shm-usage"
           ]
       }
   }
   ```

11. Run the following command to generate `phpunit.xml` file:

   ```
   php artisan dusk:install
   ```

12. Finally, run the tests by executing the following command:

   ```
   php artisan dusk
   ```

That's it! Your Laravel Dusk testing suite should now be set up and ready to use on your AWS EC2 instance.

Comparison of Operating Systems: Windows 10 and macOS Big Sur

| Category | Windows 10 | macOS Big Sur |
| -------- | -----------| ------------- |
| Compatibility with Programming Languages | ! [Windows 10 programming languages support](https://i.imgur.com/IrQ7fw1.png)
| Procedural Languages: | C, C++, Fortran, COBOL | C, C++, Swift, Objective-C, Python |
| Scripting Languages: | JavaScript, PowerShell, VBScript, Batch | JavaScript, Python, Ruby, Perl, Bash |
| OOP Languages: | C#, Java (via virtualization) | Swift, Objective-C |
| Hardware Requirements | 1 GHz processor; 1 GB RAM for 32-bit, 2 GB RAM for 64-bit; 16 GB available storage | 1.7 GHz Intel Core i5 processor; 8GB RAM; 35.5 GB available storage |
| User Interface Customization Options | Themes, Icon Packs, Taskbar customization | Themes, Dock customization, Widgets, App Exposé |
| Security Features | Windows Defender, Firewall, BitLocker, Credential Guard, Windows Hello | FileVault, Gatekeeper, Firewall, macOS Security, Two-Factor Authentication |
| System Updates | Automatic and manual updates for security, features, and drivers | Automatic and manual updates for security and features, periodic releases with significant updates |

Virtualization, Containerization, and Cloud Computing:

| Feature | Windows 10 | macOS Big Sur |
| -------- | ----------- | ------------- |
| Virtualization | Supports virtualization via Hyper-V and Virtual Machine Platform | Supports virtualization via built-in hypervisor and virtualization extensions |
| Built-in Virtualization | Hyper-V | Apple Hypervisor |
| Creating and Running Virtual Machines | PowerShell/C# (via Hyper-V) or third-party tools (e.g., VirtualBox, VMWare) | Virtualization preferences > Add (+) > New virtual machine; Command-line via VBoxManage (VirtualBox) |
| Containerization | Supports containerization via Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL) and Docker on Windows Server | Built-in support for Docker |
| Installing and Running Containers | WSL installation with installed Linux distribution; Docker installed via MSI | GUI: Docker app or Terminal via `docker run <IMAGE_NAME>` |
| Web Hosting (e.g., Cloud Computing) | Azure, Microsoft CDN | Apple CloudKit, Amazon Web Services (AWS), Google Cloud Platform (GCP) |

